Now we are ready to solve. First we find the product of the diagonals: $\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}=7*1+2*3+3*2=7+6+6\implies 7+6+6=19$. Next we compute the lengths of the diagonals:\
$$ ||\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} ||=\sqrt{7^2+2^2+3^2}=\sqrt{49+4+9}=\sqrt{62}$$ and
\
$$ ||\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}||=\sqrt{1^2+3^2+2^2}=\sqrt{1+9+4}=\sqrt{14}$$.
\[So, by the Dot Product Formula \[\cos \theta=\frac{\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}}{\left | \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \right |\left | \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} \right |}=\frac{19}{\cos \theta=\frac{\sqrt{62}\sqrt{14}}{\sqrt{7}*3}=\frac{19}{\sqrt{7}*3}=\frac{19\sqrt{7}}{7*3}=\frac{19\sqrt{7}}{21}}.\]
Therefore our answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(A)} \frac{19\sqrt{7}}{21}}.$
